Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 08:05:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 528/528] [DS4.1] Block verification ends each block at the
 dead-row cut

Adaptive verification's dead rows skip the routed MoE, so the logits of the
rows past a request's cut are not the target's. Block verification scanned
them anyway (each threshold weighs the next draft's probability), and the
insertion capped the count at the cut plus one afterwards.

The TileLang verify and resample kernels now take the cut (a compile-time
variant, read from the step's buffer, so a captured graph reads it on every
replay): a request's drafts from its cut on are -1 placeholders, which end a
block as the drafter's own do. The last live draft takes the joint ratio as
its threshold, no dead row weighs in, and the cut's row draws the bonus with
nothing masked. The outcome is block verification's of the truncated draft,
bit for bit, so the count never passes the cut plus one and the TileLang path
drops the insertion's cap. A greedy request commits what it committed with
the cap (the cut's row gives its target argmax either way).

The cut now reaches verification on every step, not only with logprobs or
sink rejection. The sink's alternates take it too: a last draw at the cut was
the bonus, so they mask no draft there. A step that can have dead rows
declares verification and resampling with the cut beside the warmup's without
it, and the capped insertion only for the methods that verify draft by draft.

Block verification with draft logits, a watermark or FP64 draws runs as the
Triton kernels, which cannot end a block at a cut: with dead rows it is
refused when the step's kernels are declared.
